-                                                    🟠 Before UI is Rendered (Component is being set up)

# ngOnChanges() 
called when there is change in Input parameter
If the component has @Input() properties, this runs first.
Purpose: Update based on incoming data.

# ngOnInit()
called when Component is initialized and has stared rendering on UI
Good place to prepare data (e.g., API calls, default values) before rendering.
UI Not Rendered Yet.

# ngDoCheck()
If a change happens in the parent, both parent and child ngDoCheck() will run.
If a change happens in the child, parent ngDoCheck() will still run because Angular runs change detection top-down.
Useful if Angular’s default check isn’t enough.

-                                                      🟢 After UI is Rendered (DOM is now visible)

# ngAfterContentInit()

# When is ngAfterContentInit() called?

It is called after the projected content has been initialized.

At this point:

You can access the content with @ContentChild or @ContentChildren.

The projected content is available in the DOM, but Angular hasn’t run change detection on it yet

Hook	                           Triggered When	                                                                       Useful For
ngAfterContentInit()	           After Angular inserts content via <ng-content>	                                       Accessing projected content with @ContentChild

# ngAfterContentChecked()

# So it's called: Immediately after ngAfterContentInit() — the first time.

# Every time:

A change is detected in any input-bound data

The parent component updates

Events (click, input, etc.) trigger change detection

Timers (setTimeout, setInterval)

Manual changes (ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges())



🔴 When Component is Removed / Destroyed
ngOnDestroy()

Called before Angular removes the component from the DOM.

Use this to cleanup (unsubscribe, clear timers, etc.)